    "body": "A Houston ship pilot has been indicted on a federal seaman’s manslaughter charge stemming from a 2024 collision in the Houston Ship Channel that killed a sailor, marking what authorities say is the first prosecution of its kind against a ship pilot in U.S. history.\nDarris Ornel Jefferson, 39, is accused of misconduct, negligence and inattention while piloting the Chinese bulk carrier Yangze 7 through the Houston Ship Channel on July 19, 2024, according to an indictment announced Wednesday.\nThe charges allege Jefferson’s actions caused the death of a crew member aboard the towing vessel Miss Peggy , which collided with the bulk carrier near Lynchburg, Texas.\nJefferson is scheduled to make his initial appearance before U.S. Magistrate Judge Christina Bryan on Nov. 24 at 2 p.m.\nIf convicted, he faces up to 10 years in federal prison and a $250,000 fine.\nThe indictment follows a National Transportation Safety Board investigation that concluded cellphone distraction and failure to maintain a proper lookout caused the fatal collision.\nNTSB investigation cited cellphone distraction\nAccording to the NTSB’s findings released Aug. 6, the Yangze 7 and Miss Peggy were both traveling inbound through the Houston Ship Channel when they collided.\nThe impact caused the Miss Peggy to capsize and sink with five crew members aboard.\nFour crew members were rescued by another vessel, including one who suffered serious injuries. The fifth crew member was later found dead inside the wreckage.\nNTSB investigators determined that the pilot directing the Yangze 7 had been engaged in a nonoperational cellphone conversation with another ship pilot for approximately 39 minutes before the collision.\nThe phone call ended just two minutes and 16 seconds before the crash.\nInvestigators concluded the conversation distracted the pilot from his navigation responsibilities, leaving him unaware of the Miss Peggy’s presence.\nThe investigation also found that neither vessel maintained a proper lookout while navigating the channel.\nThe captain of the Miss Peggy failed to detect the approaching Yangze 7 from behind, while the bulk carrier’s bridge team did not detect the towboat until it was less than one ship’s length ahead, according to the NTSB.\nThe NTSB determined that distraction and failure to maintain a proper lookout were the probable causes of the collision.\nFederal officials emphasize maritime safety\nThe criminal indictment represents an unusual escalation in federal enforcement following a fatal commercial maritime accident.\nOfficials said the case marks the first time a ship pilot has faced federal seaman’s manslaughter charges .\n“The tragic loss of life shows the serious consequences that can result when a mariner does not carry out their duties with proper care and attention,” said Damon J. Youmans, special agent in charge of the U.S. Coast Guard Investigative Service’s Gulf Field Office.\nWhy it matters: The federal criminal case against a Houston ship pilot could intensify scrutiny of navigation practices, cellphone use and accountability for maritime accidents at major U.S. shipping gateways.",
